WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.200
So that's one of the major benefits
of outputting, of speaking is

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.440
that you're getting back input
that you're very interested in.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.360
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.010
Today to talk about languages.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.650
And today I'm going to talk about output.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:14.400
All right.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.680
You'll hear me talking all the
time about the importance of input.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.470
Uh, getting the language in you.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.530
I even did a video where I said
don't speak in the language you're

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.660
learning, a bit controversial perhaps.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.050
And I'll explain why I made that
video, but of course, eventually

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.310
we want to get to output.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:30.870
We want to speak the language.

00:00:31.905 --> 00:00:34.455
Prove to ourselves that we can
say things in the language.

00:00:34.455 --> 00:00:36.555
We want to communicate with
people in the language.

00:00:36.585 --> 00:00:42.285
Output is important and the languages
that I speak the best and probably

00:00:42.285 --> 00:00:46.125
understand the best are the ones
that I have spoken the most.

00:00:46.305 --> 00:00:48.495
So you do have to get to output.

00:00:48.675 --> 00:00:50.625
So I want to talk a bit about output.

00:00:50.655 --> 00:00:50.985
Okay.

00:00:52.905 --> 00:00:55.305
If you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on

00:00:55.305 --> 00:00:56.805
the bell for notifications.

00:00:57.075 --> 00:01:01.155
Uh, follow me on your favorite
podcast service, leave a review.

00:01:01.155 --> 00:01:02.565
I do appreciate it.

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:04.815
Output.

00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:08.325
First of all, everything that
has to do with language learning

00:01:08.445 --> 00:01:10.755
is governed by the three keys.

00:01:10.815 --> 00:01:14.565
In other words, your attitude towards
learning towards the language,

00:01:14.565 --> 00:01:16.005
your interest, your confidence.

00:01:16.425 --> 00:01:20.145
Second of all, the time you spend
with the language with the language,

00:01:20.295 --> 00:01:23.145
not sitting in the classroom,
having someone explain something

00:01:23.145 --> 00:01:25.095
in English, but with the language.

00:01:25.215 --> 00:01:26.175
So that can be listening.

00:01:26.175 --> 00:01:26.985
It can be speaking.

00:01:26.985 --> 00:01:27.765
It can be writing.

00:01:27.765 --> 00:01:28.545
It can be reading.

00:01:28.785 --> 00:01:29.895
Time with the language.

00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:33.690
And of course the third thing I
always mention, the ability to

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.690
notice what's going on in the
language and I'll get into those.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:41.220
But the reason I made my video saying
don't speak in the language you're

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:47.040
learning is because there is too much
emphasis on speaking on speaking early

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.700
as if, if you don't speak right away, or
if you don't speak as you're learning the

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.350
language, you'll never be able to speak.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.760
That's not true.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.380
You can speak when you feel like speaking,
eventually you have to speak a lot.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:02.804
Some people are in a situation where
they're surrounded by the language

00:02:02.804 --> 00:02:06.554
they would like to, they have the
opportunity, they have the need to speak.

00:02:06.554 --> 00:02:07.304
They speak early.

00:02:07.634 --> 00:02:12.105
Other people as is often my case where
I'm learning here in Vancouver, I'm

00:02:12.105 --> 00:02:14.775
learning Persian or Arabic or Greek.

00:02:14.894 --> 00:02:15.674
I'm not there.

00:02:15.704 --> 00:02:16.334
I'm here.

00:02:16.454 --> 00:02:20.625
So I put most of my effort into
input because it's easy to arrange.

00:02:20.745 --> 00:02:24.355
I'm spending time with the
language while in my car.

00:02:24.534 --> 00:02:28.420
I'm spending time with the language
when I sit down with a book, so

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.130
spend time with the language.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:35.740
However, in all of those cases, after
a couple of months, I start outputting.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.825
I start speaking initially a
small amount and eventually I

00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:42.015
built it up to more speaking.

00:02:42.285 --> 00:02:44.745
And so I think there are
different kinds of speaking.

00:02:45.225 --> 00:02:50.055
Obviously, when I lived in Japan,
I could go into any store and use

00:02:50.055 --> 00:02:54.765
whatever I had in the way of words and
gestures, and I could be communicating.

00:02:55.125 --> 00:02:59.755
And every time you output, obviously
you're speaking to someone,

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:03.774
you're getting input and input
is how we acquire the language.

00:03:03.954 --> 00:03:08.364
And so you are triggering input
that's very relevant to you.

00:03:08.515 --> 00:03:12.625
So that's one of the major benefits
of outputting, of speaking is that

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:17.635
you're getting back input that
you're very interested in, but if

00:03:17.635 --> 00:03:20.755
you're not in that situation, then
you have to organize the output.

00:03:20.995 --> 00:03:26.705
You have to, uh, set up online to
find someone to talk to, which can

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.530
be done through italki or at LingQ.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:35.460
Uh, or as in my case here in Vancouver,
we have Korean speakers in Vancouver.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.230
We have Chinese speakers in Vancouver.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.580
We have, uh, Persian speakers in
Vancouver, so I can find those people.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:46.530
They might be clerks in a store or a
teller at a bank or someone sitting

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:51.970
beside me on a bus and I can initiate
this kind of spontaneous interaction

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:53.799
in the language,  which is fun to do.

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.600
However, again, the more words
you have, the more you understand

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.079
of what they're saying, the more
meaningful that interaction can be.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.019
The same is true when you're
talking to a tutor online.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:09.130
I know when I started in these languages,
whether it be Czech or Korean or whatever,

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.140
the first few sessions are very painful.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.910
I can't really understand what the
other person is saying very well.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.650
And I don't have many words.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.440
Uh, maybe I have some words
that I have passively.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.090
I haven't started using them.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:21.960
I can't find them.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.400
So it's a bit of a struggle.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.369
You simply have to persevere.

00:04:26.729 --> 00:04:30.570
And I think this is another important
point, uh, with, with language

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:31.950
learning, don't be discouraged.

00:04:31.979 --> 00:04:33.419
It's not performance.

00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:36.599
You are not a, a diver
in the Olympic games.

00:04:36.599 --> 00:04:37.890
You're not a figure skater.

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:42.090
No one is going to be grading
you I'm, I, I guess if you're in

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:45.990
a classroom, someone might grade
you, but typically that's not the

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.450
situation you're trying to communicate.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.910
You're struggling to understand
what the person is saying.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.000
You're struggling to say
something and, and the, the

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.724
objective is simply communication.

00:04:57.414 --> 00:05:01.734
And, um, on that basis, you shouldn't
be discouraged if you struggle.

00:05:01.745 --> 00:05:05.365
Because again, if we remember the
three keys, as long as you have a

00:05:05.365 --> 00:05:10.664
positive attitude and you spend time
with the language you will improve.

00:05:10.724 --> 00:05:13.724
And of course you have to notice,
and this is the other thing that

00:05:13.724 --> 00:05:17.684
happens when you speak with people,
you notice the words you're missing.

00:05:17.835 --> 00:05:20.565
You notice the difficulties
you have with grammar.

00:05:20.835 --> 00:05:26.445
Now you also notice these things when
you're listening or reading and noticing

00:05:26.505 --> 00:05:31.034
is kind of a very important part of
acquiring the language, because until you

00:05:31.034 --> 00:05:36.044
notice certain things, you aren't going
to be able to produce them in your output.

00:05:36.224 --> 00:05:40.535
So if we're focusing on noticing
while we're speaking, noticing the...

00:05:40.605 --> 00:05:44.825
mistakes we make, noticing what other
people say, we will gradually improve.

00:05:45.125 --> 00:05:48.995
One of the things that I do
at LingQ to improve my ability

00:05:48.995 --> 00:05:51.005
to notice, say conjugations.

00:05:51.365 --> 00:05:55.685
If I look up a word in LingQ,
click on it, we have a variety of

00:05:55.685 --> 00:05:57.845
dictionaries there that are available.

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.330
Uh, I don't just look up
the meaning of the word.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:04.960
If it's a verb, I'll go to one
of our conjugating dictionaries.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:09.730
It might be Context Reverso, which has
conjugating dictionaries for Greek,

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:12.100
for Arabic, for the romance languages.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:16.750
And I'll see, okay this particular
form of the verb, what is it?

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:17.410
Okay.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:20.969
And then at the same time, I look
at all the other forms of the

00:06:20.969 --> 00:06:25.620
verb and that's just gradually
helping me to notice, although I

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.179
won't remember what I saw there.

00:06:27.179 --> 00:06:30.030
It's just slowly helping me to notice.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:34.605
And so then when I next speak
with my tutor, for example, I

00:06:34.605 --> 00:06:36.585
did in, in Persian the other day.

00:06:36.855 --> 00:06:43.094
And she sort of reminded me that I don't
always use the subjunctive in Persian

00:06:43.635 --> 00:06:45.495
and I should pay more attention to that.

00:06:45.495 --> 00:06:46.485
I should notice more.

00:06:46.485 --> 00:06:48.164
So I started noticing more.

00:06:48.344 --> 00:06:52.305
And then the next time I spoke
with my tutor she told me at

00:06:52.305 --> 00:06:53.745
least that I was doing better.

00:06:54.525 --> 00:06:57.705
Bear in mind in your output activities
if you were speaking with the tutor

00:06:57.705 --> 00:07:01.035
or whatever the case may be, you
will probably continue making those

00:07:01.035 --> 00:07:02.865
same mistakes for quite a while.

00:07:03.105 --> 00:07:08.175
It's not because you notice something or
because your teacher corrects you that

00:07:08.175 --> 00:07:12.465
you can immediately develop the habits
you need to speak correctly all the time.

00:07:12.765 --> 00:07:17.655
It's part of this gradual process of
getting used to the language, noticing

00:07:17.655 --> 00:07:21.345
things, developing new habits and
so forth, but you'll need to do this

00:07:21.345 --> 00:07:23.505
in order to be good at outputting.

00:07:23.775 --> 00:07:27.359
So I'll get back to my original
statement: it doesn't matter when you

00:07:27.359 --> 00:07:30.380
start speaking, it's not as if the
language is going to go away from you,

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:31.979
you can always come back and speak.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.590
You can always review
the grammar rules again.

00:07:34.799 --> 00:07:37.020
You can always work on
improving your accuracy.

00:07:37.169 --> 00:07:40.289
The first thing is to let the
brain get used to the language

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:41.760
without worrying about it.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:46.890
And so when you speak, you just let it all
hang out, say whatever you're able to say,

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:49.140
listen to what the other person is saying.

00:07:49.409 --> 00:07:52.905
Hopefully you're
gathering some new habits.

00:07:53.205 --> 00:07:58.155
Mostly I find when I'm in that situation
at an early stage in my outputting,

00:07:58.335 --> 00:08:00.225
I'm just thinking about words.

00:08:00.465 --> 00:08:03.515
I can get the wrong tense, the
wrong person it doesn't matter.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.460
The meaning comes across because language
is quite redundant, but I need words.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:13.160
I need to know the verb
for want, need, go, come.

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:15.750
I need to know I need the
words first and foremost.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:21.660
And that's my initial goal in outputting
is to somehow muster the vocabulary

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:25.260
that I have been accumulating passively
and try to use it in some way.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:30.800
And I'll use it incorrectly, but
gradually as I continue outputting

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:35.689
continue noticing, continue with
my input activities I gradually

00:08:35.689 --> 00:08:37.760
develop better and better habits.

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:40.760
So basically on output, that's my advice.

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:42.890
Focus on your input activities.

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.680
Make sure you stay positive attitude.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:48.110
Makes sure you spend
time with the language.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:52.560
Listening, reading and speaking,
try to notice what's happening.

00:08:52.589 --> 00:08:56.490
Try to notice your mistakes, try to
notice structures and words that you

00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:01.199
have trouble with and just keep doing
it, keep enjoying it and gradually

00:09:01.199 --> 00:09:03.630
your output ability will improve.

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:05.400
So I hope that was useful.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.329
And maybe a bit of a countermeasure
to the video that I put on earlier

00:09:09.329 --> 00:09:12.180
about not speaking in your, in
the language you're learning.

00:09:12.209 --> 00:09:12.660
Yes.

00:09:12.689 --> 00:09:15.719
Do speak in the language you're
learning when you feel like it.

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:17.700
And don't worry about how you do.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.365
Thank you for listening.

00:09:19.425 --> 00:09:19.815
Bye for now.

